Z||000004||Raydar||10-11-2001||09:16 AM||||Haze,<P>You'll have to find locks that use the same "blank" (uncut key). Door locks and trunk locks use different blanks. You might be able to mix and match between years, but I'm not sure. <BR>I read an article somewhere that described how to modify one type of blank to work in both types of locks. Involved grinding down a ridge or cutting a lengthwise slot, if I remember correctly. If I can find it, I'll pass it along.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||10-11-2001||02:20 PM||||dealer or lock shop. all they do is change the positions of the tumblers in cylinder. In fact you could get all the tumblers in all your GM cars lock cylinders to match and use one set of keys in all of them. Ignition though would be a problem.||||reg||1
